                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n(If no tile is placed – i.e. “one = 0” – the condition that each row and each column contain exactly one uncovered unit square is violated, because every row and column would contain 2025 uncovered squares. Hence the case of zero tiles does **not** satisfy the constraints.)\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Model the problem with a matrix.**  \n   Let \\(n=2025\\).  After the placement of tiles, the uncovered squares form a set of exactly one square in each row and each column; thus they correspond to a permutation matrix \\(P\\).  The matrix of covered squares is \\(M=J-P\\), where \\(J\\) is the all‑ones \\(n\\times n\\) matrix.\n\n2. **Lower bound via rank.**  \n   Each rectangular tile corresponds to a rank‑1 binary matrix (the outer product of the characteristic vectors of its row set and column set).  A partition of the covered squares into \\(m\\) tiles yields a decomposition  \n   \\[\n   M=\\sum_{k=1}^{m}R_k,\n   \\]\n   where each \\(R_k\\) has rank 1.  Hence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le m\\).  Since \\(M=J-P\\) is invertible, \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n\\).  Consequently \\(m\\ge n\\).\n\n3. **Upper bound by an explicit construction.**  \n   For each row \\(i\\) let the uncovered square be at column \\(p(i)\\).  Place a horizontal tile covering the whole row \\(i\\) except the square \\((i,p(i))\\).  This tile has dimensions \\(1\\times (n-1)\\).  The \\(n\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint (they lie in distinct rows) and together cover every square that is not uncovered.  Thus \\(m\\le n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the lower bound \\(m\\ge n\\) with the construction achieving \\(m=n\\) gives the exact minimum \\(m=n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Formalisation\n\nAfter the tiles are placed, each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) matrix  \n\n\\[\nM=(m_{ij}),\\qquad \nm_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n0 & \\text{if }j=\\pi(i) \\;(i,j)\\in U,\\\\[2mm]\n1 & \\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(M\\) has a zero in each row and column exactly at the position prescribed by the permutation \\(\\pi\\); all other entries are \\(1\\).  In matrix notation  \n\n\\[\nM=J-P,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix and \\(P\\) is the permutation matrix of \\(\\pi\\).\n\nA rectangular tile whose set of rows is \\(A\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and whose set of columns is \\(B\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) covers precisely the entries \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in A,\\;j\\in B\\}\\).  The corresponding binary matrix is  \n\n\\[\nR_{A,B}= \\mathbf{1}_A\\,\\mathbf{1}_B^{\\!\\top},\n\\]\n\nthe outer product of the characteristic vectors of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Hence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(R_{A,B})=1\\).\n\nA placement of tiles that covers every \\(1\\) of \\(M\\) without overlap yields a decomposition  \n\n\\[\nM=\\sum_{k=1}^{m}R_{A_k,B_k},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhere each pair \\((A_k,B_k)\\) describes one tile, the sets \\(\\{A_k\\times B_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and the sum is taken over the integers (no entry exceeds \\(1\\) because the tiles are disjoint).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nFrom (1) we obtain, using the subadditivity of matrix rank,\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\operatorname{rank}(R_{A_k,B_k})\n =\\sum_{k=1}^{m}1=m.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(m\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\).\n\nThe matrix \\(M=J-P\\) is invertible.  Indeed, the eigenvalues of \\(J\\) are \\(n\\) (once) and \\(0\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)); the eigenvalues of \\(P\\) are all roots of unity, in particular \\(1\\) appears exactly once.  Consequently the eigenvalues of \\(M\\) are \\(n-1\\) (once) and \\(-1\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)), none of which is zero.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n.\n\\]\n\nCombining this with (2) gives the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge n.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nLet the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) be at column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  For each row \\(i\\) place a horizontal tile\n\n\\[\nT_i = \\{i\\}\\times\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\pi(i)\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe tile \\(T_i\\) has dimensions \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) and covers all squares of row \\(i\\) except the unique uncovered one \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  The tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows.  Their union is precisely the set of all \\(1\\)’s of \\(M\\).  Hence this construction uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles and satisfies all requirements.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nm\\le n.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Minimality\n\nFrom (3) and (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nm=n.\n\\]\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\), the minimum number of tiles required is therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  The case of zero tiles\n\nIf no tile is placed (\\(m=0\\)), every unit square remains uncovered.  Consequently each row (and each column) contains \\(2025\\) uncovered squares, contradicting the problem’s condition that there must be **exactly one** uncovered square per row and per column.  Hence the configuration with zero tiles does **not** satisfy the constraints."
