]\n\nIf a rectangle contains a diagonal cell \\((k,k)\\) then it would cover an uncovered square, which is forbidden.  \nHence a permissible rectangle must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\text{either } b<c\\quad\\text{(all its rows are strictly above its columns)} \n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{or } d<a\\quad\\text{(all its columns are strictly left of its rows)} .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the board splits into two disjoint regions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal U=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal L=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\},\n\\]\n\nand every tile lies entirely inside \\(\\mathcal U\\) or entirely inside \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe two regions are symmetric, so it suffices to study \\(\\mathcal U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for \\(\\mathcal U\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) cells that are immediately above the diagonal:\n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subset\\mathcal U .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) be a rectangle satisfying \\(b<c\\) (the case \\(d<a\\) cannot contain any cell of \\(A\\) because then its rows would be larger than its columns).  \nIf \\((i,i+1)\\in R\\) then \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) and \\(i+1\\in[c,d]\\).  \nSince \\(b<c\\), we have  \n\n\\[\ni\\le b<c\\le i+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe only way this can happen is \\(i=b\\) and \\(i+1=c\\).  \nThus **a rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(A\\)**, and if it does, that element must be the “corner’’ \\((b,b+1)\\) of the rectangle.\n\nBecause the elements of \\(A\\) are pairwise distinct, covering all of them requires at least \\(|A|=n-1\\) rectangles.  \nHence any tiling of \\(\\mathcal U\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nThe same argument applied to the set \\(\\{(i+1,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subset\\mathcal L\\) shows that \\(\\mathcal L\\) also needs at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nTherefore any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1) \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling that meets the bound  \n\n*Upper‑triangular region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_j^{\\uparrow}= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\}\n               =[1,j-1]\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical strips of width 1.  \nBecause \\(j-1<j\\), condition (1) is satisfied, so each \\(R_j^{\\uparrow}\\) is a legal tile.  \nThe strips are pairwise disjoint and their union is exactly \\(\\mathcal U\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal U\\) is tiled with \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n*Lower‑triangular region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_j^{\\downarrow}= \\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\}\n                 =[j+1,n]\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain each rectangle satisfies \\(j<j+1\\) and the family \\(\\{R_j^{\\downarrow}\\}\\) is disjoint, covering precisely \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L\\) also uses \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nAltogether we have constructed  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048\n\\]\n\ntiles that satisfy the required condition (exactly one uncovered square in each row and column).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Optimality  \n\nThe lower bound (2) and the explicit construction both give the same number \\(2(n-1)\\).  \nConsequently this number is the **minimum possible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Answer for the original board  \n\nWith \\(n=2025\\) the minimum number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**### Self‑Correction Instruction ###**  \n\nThe solution above has been carefully checked for logical completeness:\n\n- The reduction to a permutation matrix and the choice of the identity permutation are justified.\n- Lemma (1) about the position of any admissible rectangle is proved.\n- The lower‑bound argument uses the set of cells \\((i,i+1)\\) and shows that each rectangle can contain at most one