                "content": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is  \n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Notation:** Let \\(n=2025\\). The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix \\(\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) where each row \\(i\\) has a unique uncovered column \\(p(i)\\).  \n2. **Lower bound:**  \n   - Consider the cells to the **right** of each uncovered square: for row \\(i\\) these are \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>p(i)\\). Their union is a shape \\(S_R\\).  \n   - In every non‑empty row of \\(S_R\\) the rightmost cell \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) is a *corner cell*. A rectangle contained in \\(S_R\\) can contain at most one corner cell (otherwise its bottom row would have to be two different rows).  \n   - Exactly \\(n-1\\) rows have \\(p(i)<n\\); hence \\(S_R\\) has \\(n-1\\) corner cells and needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n   - The same argument applied to the cells **left** of each uncovered square gives another region \\(S_L\\) that also needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n   - Since a rectangle cannot intersect both \\(S_R\\) and \\(S_L\\) (it would contain a hole), the total number of tiles is at least \\(2(n-1)=2n-2\\).  \n3. **Upper bound (construction):**  \n   - Choose the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal: \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   - The region above the diagonal consists of the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\). Partition it into the \\(n-1\\) vertical strips  \n     \\[\n     R_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\\qquad j=2,\\dots ,n .\n     \\]  \n     Each \\(R_j\\) is a rectangle (height \\(j-1\\), width \\(1\\)) and contains no diagonal cell.  \n   - The region below the diagonal consists of the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\). Partition it into the \\(n-1\\) vertical strips  \n     \\[\n     L_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,n-1 .\n     \\]  \n     Each \\(L_j\\) is a rectangle (height \\(n-j\\), width \\(1\\)) and also avoids all diagonal cells.  \n   - Altogether we obtain \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles that cover every non‑hole unit square exactly once.  \n\nSince the lower bound equals the construction, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\).  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet the grid have size \\(n\\times n\\) with \\(n=2025\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix:\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nDenote by \\(C\\) the set of all unit squares that are covered by tiles; thus  \n\\(C=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\).\n\nA tile is an axis‑aligned rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; equivalently a tile is a submatrix\n\\[\nR\\times C=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(R\\subseteq[n]\\) and \\(C\\subseteq[n]\\) are **intervals** of consecutive indices.\nThe family of tiles must partition \\(C\\); i.e. the rectangles are pairwise disjoint and their union equals \\(C\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) write \\(p(i)\\) for the column of its uncovered square.  \nDefine the **right region**\n\\[\nS_R=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in[n],\\;j>p(i)\\},\n\\]\nand the **left region**\n\\[\nS_L=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in[n],\\;j<p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nClearly \\(C=S_R\\cup S_L\\) and the two sets are disjoint.\n\nConsider \\(S_R\\).  \nIn any row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) the rightmost cell of \\(S_R\\) is\n\\[\nc_i=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\]\nThese cells are called **corner cells** of \\(S_R\\).  \nIf a rectangle \\(R\\times C\\subseteq S_R\\) contains two distinct corner cells\n\\(c_{i}\\) and \\(c_{i'}\\) with \\(i<i'\\), then its bottom row must be both \\(i\\) (because the\nrightmost column of the rectangle equals \\(p(i)+1\\)) and \\(i'\\) (by the same argument for\n\\(c_{i'}\\)), a contradiction.  Hence **each rectangle of a partition of \\(S_R\\) can contain at most one corner cell**.\n\nExactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=n\\); all other \\(n-1\\) rows satisfy \\(p(i)<n\\).  \nThus \\(S_R\\) possesses \\(n-1\\) distinct corner cells, and consequently any rectangle\npartition of \\(S_R\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nThe same reasoning applied to \\(S_L\\) (now the corner cells are \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) for the\n\\(n-1\\) rows with \\(p(i)>1\\)) shows that any rectangle partition of \\(S_L\\) also needs at\nleast \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nA rectangle cannot intersect both \\(S_R\\) and \\(S_L\\) because any such rectangle would\ncontain a point of \\(U\\) (the uncovered square lying between the left and right parts).\nHence the total number of rectangles in a partition of \\(C\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal\n\\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Upper region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define the vertical strip\n\\[\nR_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\\(R_j\\) is a rectangle of height \\(j-1\\) and width \\(1\\); it lies entirely above the\ndiagonal, hence contains no uncovered square.  \nThe family \\(\\{R_j\\mid j=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles whose\nunion is exactly the set \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\).\n\n*Lower region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define the vertical strip\n\\[\nL_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(L_j\\) is a rectangle of height \\(n-j\\) and width \\(1\\); it lies entirely below the\ndiagonal and avoids all uncovered squares.  \nThe family \\(\\{L_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consists of another \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles\nwhose union is \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}\\).\n\nTogether the \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_j\\}\\cup\\{L_j\\}\\) cover every unit square of the\ngrid except the diagonal squares, and no two rectangles overlap.  Hence a tiling with\nexactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (1) shows that any admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nThe construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we obtain\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
