-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     L_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n     which lies entirely below the diagonal.  \n\n   The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_j\\}\\) and the \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{L_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, avoid all diagonal squares, and together cover every non\u2011diagonal unit square.  Thus a tiling with exactly \\(2(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently there exists a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares are to be covered by axis\u2011aligned rectangular tiles; each tile is a set  \n\n\\[\nT=I\\times J=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in I,\\;j\\in J\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are intervals of consecutive indices.  \nThe family of tiles must be pairwise disjoint and its union must equal  \n\n\\[\nC=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U,\n\\]\n\nthe set of covered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A monotone lattice path and corner squares  \n\nConsider the grid lines of the \\(n\\times n\\) board.  \nStarting at the point \\((0,n)\\) (just left of the top\u2011left corner) we trace a lattice path \\(P\\) as follows:\n\n```\nfor i = 1,2,\u2026,n:\n    move right until the vertical line x = p(i) is reached,\n    then move down one unit.\n```\n\nThe path \\(P\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) right steps and \\(n\\) down steps, hence it joins \\((0,n)\\) to \\((n,0)\\).  \nAt each interior turn of \\(P\\) the path changes direction from right to down or from down to right.  \nThe unit square that lies immediately to the *outside* of the turn (i.e. the square sharing the turning corner but not containing an uncovered point) is a **corner square**.  \n\nThere are \\(2n-2\\) interior turns, therefore exactly \\(2n-2\\) distinct corner squares.  \nAll corner squares belong to \\(C\\) (they are covered), because a turn occurs precisely at a grid line adjacent to an uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\) but never inside it.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound: one corner per tile  \n\nLet \\(T=I\\times J\\) be a tile that contains no uncovered square.  \nSuppose, for a contradiction, that \\(T\\) contains two distinct corner squares.\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 both are right\u2011turn corners.*  \nA right\u2011turn corner has the form \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) (it exists whenever \\(p(i)<n\\)).  \nIf \\(T\\) contains two such corners \\((i_1,p(i_1)+1)\\) and \\((i_2,p(i_2)+1)\\) with \\(i_1<i_2\\), then the column interval \\(J\\) contains both \\(p(i_1)+1\\) and \\(p(i_2)+1\\).  \nSince \\(p(i_1)<p(i_2)\\) (otherwise the two columns would coincide, contradicting the bijectivity of \\(p\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\np(i_1)+1\\le p(i_2)\\le\\max J .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered square \\((i_2,p(i_2))\\) lies in \\(I\\times J\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(T\\) avoids uncovered squares.\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 both are left\u2011turn corners.*  \nA left\u2011turn corner has the form \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) (it exists whenever \\(p(i)>1\\)).  \nAn identical argument shows that a rectangle containing two left\u2011turn corners would also contain an uncovered square.\n\n*Case 3 \u2013 one right\u2011turn corner \\((i_1,p(i_1)+1)\\) and one left\u2011turn corner \\((i_2,p(i_2)-1)\\).*  \nAssume \\(i_1\\le i_2\\).  \nBecause the column interval \\(J\\) contains both \\(p(i_1)+1\\) and \\(p(i_2)-1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\np(i_1)+1\\le p(i_2)-1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p(i_1)<p(i_2).\n\\]\n\nConsequently the uncovered square \\((i_2,p(i_2))\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\np(i_1)+1\\le p(i_2)-1 < p(i_2)\\le\\max J,\n\\]\n\nso \\((i_2,p(i_2))\\in I\\times J\\), again a contradiction.\n\nAll possibilities lead to a contradiction; therefore **any tile can contain at most one corner square**.\n\nSince there are exactly \\(2n-2\\) corner squares, any covering of all squares of \\(C\\) by tiles requires at least  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the lower bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R_j\\) consists of the vertical strip of column \\(j\\) lying **above** the diagonal; it contains no diagonal square.\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nL_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_j\\) is the vertical strip of column \\(j\\) lying **below** the diagonal; it also avoids all diagonal squares.\n\nThe families \\(\\{R_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) and \\(\\{L_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, each rectangle respects the grid lines, and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal cell of the board.  \nThe number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{R_j\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{L_j\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower\u2011bound argument shows that no admissible tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
