]\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nConsequently each positive integer \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) occurs exactly once among the \\(\\ell_i\\)\u2019s and exactly once among the \\(r_i\\)\u2019s.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1 (left side).  \nAny tiling of \\(C\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles that lie **entirely to the left** of the uncovered squares of the rows they intersect.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a rectangle \\(R\\) that contains only squares left of the uncovered squares of the rows it covers.  \nSince the sides of a rectangle are on grid lines, \\(R\\) must be of the form  \n\n\\[\nR=I\\times\\{1,\\dots ,c\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(I\\subseteq[n]\\) is a non\u2011empty set of rows and \\(c\\) is an integer satisfying  \n\n\\[\nc<\\sigma(i)\\qquad\\text{for every }i\\in I.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(c\\le\\ell_i\\) for all \\(i\\in I\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nL_k:=\\{\\,i\\in[n]\\mid \\ell_i\\ge k\\,\\}\\qquad(k=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(L_k\\) are nested: \\(L_1\\supseteq L_2\\supseteq\\cdots\\supseteq L_{n-1}\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(k\\) the squares in column \\(k\\) belonging to \\(C\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\, (i,k)\\mid i\\in L_k \\,\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAny rectangle covering a square of column \\(k\\) must have its rightmost column at least \\(k\\); by (1) such a rectangle can involve only rows from \\(L_k\\).  \nIf a rectangle has rightmost column **strictly larger** than \\(k\\), then it cannot contain any row \\(i\\) with \\(\\ell_i=k\\) (otherwise column \\(k+1\\) would lie to the left of the uncovered square in that row, contradicting the definition of \\(\\ell_i\\)).  \nHence the rows with \\(\\ell_i=k\\) (there is exactly one such row) must be covered by a rectangle whose rightmost column is **exactly** \\(k\\).  \n\nSince the values \\(\\ell_i\\) run through all integers \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\) without repetition, we obtain a distinct required rectangle for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011hand rectangles are necessary. \u220e\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2 (right side).  \nAnalogously, any tiling of \\(C\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles that lie **entirely to the right** of the uncovered squares of the rows they intersect.\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine \\(r_i=n-\\sigma(i)\\) as above.  \nA rectangle that lies to the right of the uncovered squares of the rows it covers must be of the form  \n\n\\[\nR=I\\times\\{d,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(d>\\sigma(i)\\) for all \\(i\\in I\\), i.e. \\(d\\ge n-r_i+1\\).  \nRepeating the argument of Lemma\u202f1 with the sets  \n\n\\[\nR_k:=\\{\\,i\\in[n]\\mid r_i\\ge k\\,\\}\\qquad(k=1,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nshows that for each \\(k\\) there must be a rectangle whose leftmost column is exactly \\(n-k+1\\).  \nSince the numbers \\(r_i\\) also run through \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\) once each, we need at least \\(n-1\\) right\u2011hand rectangles. \u220e\n\n\n\n##### Corollary (global lower bound).  \nAny tiling of the board satisfying the problem\u2019s conditions uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nTake the **identity permutation** \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThus the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(i\\in[n]\\).\n\n*Left\u2011hand rectangles.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_k:=\\{\\,i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,1,\\dots ,k\\,\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIn row \\(i\\ge k+1\\) we have \\(\\ell_i=i-1\\ge k\\); therefore the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) are exactly the left\u2011hand squares of that row, and they belong to \\(L_k\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, avoid the diagonal (because for any \\((i,j)\\in L_k\\) we have \\(j\\le k<i\\)), and together cover **all** squares left of the diagonal.\n\n*Right\u2011hand rectangles.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_k:=\\{\\,i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-k\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIn row \\(i\\le n-k\\) we have \\(r_i=n-i\\ge k\\); consequently the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\ge n-k+1\\) are precisely the right\u2011hand squares of that row, and they belong to \\(R_k\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1}\\) are also pairwise disjoint, avoid the diagonal (because for any \\((i,j)\\in R_k\\) we have \\(j>i\\)), and together cover **all** squares right of the diagonal.\n\n*Disjointness of left and right families.*  \nIf a cell belonged to both some \\(L_{k}\\) and some \\(R_{\\ell}\\), we would have simultaneously  \n\n\\[\nj\\le k\\quad\\text{and}\\quad j\\ge n-\\ell+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich implies \\(k\\ge n-\\ell+1\\).  \nBut then the row index would satisfy  \n\n\\[\ni\\ge k+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad i\\le n-\\ell,\n\\]\n\nso \\(k+1\\le n-\\ell\\), i.e. \\(k+\\ell\\le n-1\\).  \nTogether with \\(k\\ge n-\\ell+1\\) we obtain \\(n-\\ell+1\\le k\\le n-\\ell-1\\), a contradiction.  \nHence the two families are disjoint.\n\nThus the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) form a valid tiling: they are disjoint, avoid the diagonal uncovered squares, and together cover every non\u2011diagonal square of the board.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound of Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nThe construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are sufficient.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\#\\text{tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
